course: dictionary
lesson: 9147
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. «At every handover Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other, and Tanner stares at the floor.» Mums and dads do not shoot at each other, so snipe must mean something else here. The clues are the words each other and the brackets.
explain: The brackets say (little sharp, mean remarks). So to snipe at someone means to make small, nasty comments about them. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the sentence? If it does, you have probably found the right one.
explain: In a nature book, snipe means a bird. A snipe is a wading bird with a very long, straight beak. The clues would be words like marsh, mud and beak. Check the clues each time, because the same word can mean two different things.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and context tells you which one is meant.
fact: «Snipe» can be a noun: a wading bird with a very long, straight beak that lives in marshes and wet fields.
fact: «Snipe» can be a verb: to shoot at people from a hidden place far away.
fact: Informal meanings: «to snipe at someone» means to make nasty, sly little criticisms, and «to snipe» in an online auction or game means to win at the last second.
fact: The word «sniper» comes from hunting snipe, which were so quick that only good shooters could hit them.
exercise: «At every handover Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other.» What does snipe mean here? | *To make little sharp, mean remarks | To shoot from a hidden place far away | To wade through the marsh looking for worms | To win at the last second
exercise: A snipe probes the mud with its long beak. What kind of thing is a snipe? | A teacher who criticises | *A wading bird | A type of gun | A computer game
exercise: Which clue tells you snipe means unkind remarks in «Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other»? | The word stares | *The brackets saying little sharp, mean remarks | The word handover | The name Tanner
test: In a nature book, what is a snipe? | A boat for crossing a lake | *A wading bird with a very long, straight beak | A gun used by soldiers | A fox that hides in the grass
test: In a game, what does «to snipe» mean? | To swim across a lake | To hide in a shed | To sing quietly | *To win at the last second
exam: In «Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other», what does snipe mean? | *To make little sharp, mean remarks | To win at the last second | To shoot from far away | To probe the mud for worms
